@page "/dashboard/connect"
@inherits DashboardPageBase

<PageTitle>Connect · Landbridge</PageTitle>

@if (RefuseReason is { } reason)
{
    <ScopeCard Reason="@reason" />
}
else if (Ready)
{
    <h1>Connect</h1>
    <p class="sub">How a Lead reaches this plane, and how a new machine joins the fleet.</p>

    <section class="howto">
        <h2>Connect as a Lead</h2>
        <p>A Lead is a harness the operator drives. It talks to this plane over MCP
            with a <span class="mono">lbr_l_…</span> token. The dashboard login is a
            <em>human</em> session. The Lead token is a factory, not a Team — every
            Lead tool except <span class="mono">create_team</span> and
            <span class="mono">list_profiles</span> takes a <span class="mono">teamId</span>.</p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                Issue a Lead token with the form below (operator / human session only).
                That mints a <span class="mono">lbr_l_…</span> factory token and assigns the first
                Team (leave the id blank to start a new one). One live owner per Team;
                a second person is refused unless they take over.
            </li>
            <li>
                Point the harness at this plane with that token as a static bearer.
                Call <span class="mono">create_team</span> for another Team, or pass a
                human-supplied team id. Do not write the id into the project. The MCP endpoint is <span class="mono">POST @McpUrl</span> (the origin,
                not <span class="mono">/mcp</span>). Grok (and any client that only
                speaks Dynamic Client Registration) cannot complete OAuth here —
                this plane has no <span class="mono">/register</span>; clients are
                identified by a Client ID Metadata Document. For Grok, put the token in
                the header:
                <pre class="howto">[mcp_servers.landbridge]
url = "@McpUrl"
enabled = true

[mcp_servers.landbridge.headers]
Authorization = "Bearer lbr_l_…"</pre>
                Then refresh <span class="mono">/mcps</span> (or restart Grok).
            </li>
            <li>
                A CIMD client (Claude Code) can OAuth instead: authorization code +
                PKCE S256, same operator passphrase as this dashboard. That mints a
                <span class="mono">lbr_h_…</span> human session, not a Lead — still
                issue a Lead token here and send the <span class="mono">lbr_l_…</span> token.
                Discovery is
                <span class="mono">/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource</span> and
                <span class="mono">/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server</span>.
            </li>
            <li>
                Read <span class="mono">landbridge://skills/lead</span> from
                <span class="mono">resources/list</span>. Call
                <span class="mono">list_profiles</span>, then
                <span class="mono">create_session</span> with an exact name that came
                back and that <span class="mono">teamId</span>. There is no reserved
                <span class="mono">default</span>.
            </li>
        </ol>
        @if (Principal is Principal.Lead)
        {
            <p>This session is a Lead factory. Teams it owns:
                @if (TeamScope is { Count: > 0 })
                {
                    @foreach (var id in TeamScope)
                    {
                        <TeamLink TeamId="@id" Slug="@(TeamSlugs.GetValueOrDefault(id))" />
                    }
                }
                else
                {
                    <span class="nt">none yet — call create_team.</span>
                }
                Paste that same token into a harness, or stay here and drive from the Team view.
                New agents should call create_team rather than reuse an id from this page.</p>
        }
        else if (Principal is Principal.Human)
        {
            <form class="card connect-claim" method="post" action="/dashboard/connect/claim">
                <label for="teamId">Team id</label>
                <input id="teamId" type="text" name="teamId" class="mono"
                       spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off"
                       placeholder="leave blank to start a new Team" />
                <label class="check">
                    <input type="checkbox" name="takeover" value="true" />
                    Take over if this Team already has a Lead
                </label>
                <p class="nt">Issue Lead token shows it once. One-time setup link
                    mints a URL whose first GET is markdown that contains the bearer
                    — paste that URL to an agent; do not open it yourself. A takeover
                    evicts the incumbent; their next call is told who did it.</p>
                <button type="submit">Issue Lead token</button>
                <button type="submit" formaction="/dashboard/connect/setup-link">
                    One-time setup link
                </button>
            </form>
        }
    </section>

    <section class="howto">
        <h2>Enroll a machine</h2>
        <p><span class="mono">landbridged</span> dials out. Enrollment exchanges a
            single-use, 15-minute token for persistent machine credentials. Never put
            the token on argv — it would land in shell history and
            <span class="mono">/proc/…/cmdline</span>.</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Issue a token with the form below (operator only).</li>
            <li>
                On the box, write it to a <span class="mono">0600</span> file and exchange it.
                <span class="mono">--name</span> is the Machine Group label (defaults to hostname),
                not a runner profile.
                <pre class="howto">landbridged --enroll \
  --control-url @McpUrl \
  --enroll-token-file ./enroll.token \
  --name $(hostname -s) \
  --state-dir /var/lib/landbridged

# or pipe, no file (same --state-dir, or omit both to use ~/.landbridge):
landbridged --enroll --control-url @McpUrl --state-dir /var/lib/landbridged &lt; enroll.token</pre>
                That POSTs <span class="mono">/enroll</span> and writes
                <span class="mono">credentials.json</span> under the state dir
                (<span class="mono">0600</span> in a <span class="mono">0700</span> directory).
                Treat that file as the machine: a copy is a working identity on any host.
            </li>
            <li>
                Write a runner config. Spawn is the ACP entry point
                (<span class="mono">claude-agent-acp</span>,
                <span class="mono">codex-acp</span>,
                <span class="mono">grok agent stdio</span>,
                <span class="mono">opencode acp</span>,
                <span class="mono">goose acp</span>).
                Name the first <em>profile</em> <span class="mono">&lt;harness&gt;-&lt;hostname&gt;-&lt;os&gt;</span>
                (that is what <span class="mono">create_session</span> matches, not
                <span class="mono">--name</span>)
                and offer a group name (<span class="mono">any-linux</span> /
                <span class="mono">any-macos</span> /
                <span class="mono">any-windows</span>).
                <span class="mono">prompt</span> and <span class="mono">follow_up</span>
                must spell landbridge tools the way <em>this</em> harness does.
                Do not put bypass / always-approve on <span class="mono">spawn</span>.
                Read <span class="mono">landbridge://skills/enroll</span> and
                <span class="mono">landbridge://skills/runner-config</span> —
                there is no <span class="mono">/landbridge-enroll</span> slash command.
            </li>
            <li>
                Start the daemon with the <em>same</em> <span class="mono">--state-dir</span> you enrolled with
                (omit both to use <span class="mono">~/.landbridge</span>):
                <pre class="howto">landbridged --config /etc/landbridged/config.json --state-dir /var/lib/landbridged</pre>
                <span class="mono">landbridged</span> reads the config once, at start.
                A rewrite is invisible until you restart; a restart kills every agent
                on the box and their sessions go <span class="mono">health=failed</span>.
            </li>
            <li>
                Confirm the machine and its profile badges on
                <a href="/dashboard/machines">Machines</a>, then run a
                <a href="/dashboard/conformance">profile check</a> against each name.
                A badge is not a passing check. When you are done,
                <span class="mono">park_session</span> or
                <span class="mono">stop_session</span> the dummies — a report keeps
                the process. For a human forward later, GET
                <span class="mono">http://127.0.0.1:19378</span> on that box for the
                machine id <span class="mono">bind_machine</span> wants.
            </li>
        </ol>
        @if (Principal is Principal.Human)
        {
            <form class="card connect-enroll" method="post" action="/dashboard/connect/enroll-token">
                <p class="nt">The token is shown once. 15 minutes, single-use.
                    The Aspire loop already enrolled its three seed boxes; this is
                    for a machine that is not in that set.</p>
                <button type="submit">Issue enrollment token</button>
            </form>
        }
        else
        {
            <p class="nt">Issuing an enrollment token is a human-operator action.
                Ask your operator; a Lead token cannot mint one.</p>
        }
    </section>
}

@code {
    [Inject] private IConfiguration Config { get; set; } = default!;

    protected override bool AutoRefresh => false;

    private string McpUrl { get; set; } = DispatchService.DefaultPublicMcpUrl;

    protected override Task<string?> LoadAsync()
    {
        McpUrl = ConnectEndpoints.ResolveMcpUrl(Config, Http.HttpContext);
        return Task.FromResult<string?>(null);
    }
}
